marco: FYI QA hit a funky case in onboarding sample: rapid network flaps + auth refresh cause duplicate uploads and occasional progress stalls on Pixel 6.

aya (sdk): duplicate uploads how? two network requests for same file or one retried twice?

marco: two requests. flow: initial upload -> 401 -> Authenticator triggers refresh -> we retry the original call from Authenticator -> also our app-level retry/backoff kicks in because progress callback didn't see expected bytes -> app issues another upload. results: duplicate object on server and double charge.

nina (android): are you returning a new Request from Authenticator or cloning the original call at app layer?

marco: Authenticator returns a new request with refreshed header. app-layer has a guard but it's keyed by in-flight call id which isn't set early enough.

aya (sdk): classic race. two orthogonal retry systems: OkHttp Authenticator + app retry/backoff. Recommend one control plane for retries. Options: 1) make Authenticator return null and surface 401 to app so app can coalesce and retry once; 2) keep Authenticator but ensure app retry checks a centralized in-flight map and uses call.hash or unique upload id created before network starts.

nina (android): also, streaming RequestBody can be non-repeatable, so if OkHttp retries internally you'll get failures. Are you buffering the file or streaming it?

marco: streaming to save memory. we also observed progress stalls when the device switches networks during refresh (cell <-> wifi). UI shows spinner stuck and progress callbacks stop for ~1-3s.

aya (sdk): couple of fixes: a) make uploads idempotent on server (preferred long term) b) make app create an upload-id prior to starting and send it as metadata header so duplicates can be deduped c) for immediate mitigation, coalesce refreshes in app and avoid Authenticator doing synchronous refresh on OkHttp client used for uploads. create small separate auth client.

nina (android): code sketch for coalescer (Kotlin):

```
private val refreshMutex = Mutex()
private var refreshingToken: Deferred<String>? = null
suspend fun refreshTokenOnce(): String = coroutineScope {
  refreshingToken?.let { return@coroutineScope it.await() }
  refreshMutex.withLock {
    refreshingToken?.let { return@withLock it.await() }
    val d = async { authApi.refresh().token }
    refreshingToken = d
    try {
      return d.await()
    } finally {
      refreshingToken = null
    }
  }
}
```

aya (sdk): great. small note: do NOT call this from within Authenticator (which is synchronous). Instead let Authenticator return null so the 401 surfaces to caller coroutine; then have caller run `refreshTokenOnce()` and retry with `call.clone()`. This avoids deadlocks and lets you control retries.

marco: that fits our architecture. any quick suggestions for the progress stall?

nina (android): ensure your RequestBody checks a cancellation flag and that you close the source in the cancel path. Example: keep a `val cancelled = AtomicBoolean(false)` and in writeTo() check between reads; on cancel set flag and `source.close()`. Also post progress via `post()` and coalesce small increments so UI thread isn't flooded when network reorders bytes.

aya (sdk): add-on: document sample app with two files: `CancellableStreamingRequestBody` and `auth-coalescer` plus a note about separate small OkHttpClient for auth. We'll include a unit/integration test that emulates 401+redirect+network switch.

